# Payments and the payment provider simulator, in the order a reader would try them. Start the stack
# (docker compose up -d --wait) and open this file in VS Code with the REST Client extension, or in a JetBrains IDE.
# Every Payments request carries api-version; the simulator's calls do not, because it is somebody else's API.
#
# Payments has one HTTP door for travellers, tokenising a card, and one for operators, reading a payment. Authorising,
# capturing, voiding and refunding are requests Booking puts on Kafka, so they have no request here. To watch one happen
# without Booking, put the event on booking.v1 by hand and read payments.v1; docs/services/payments.md draws the flow.
#
# The people are in the Keycloak realm: traveller1 and admin1. Their password is "viatica".

@payments = http://localhost:8106
@psp = http://localhost:8202
@keycloak = http://localhost:8080/realms/viatica/protocol/openid-connect/token
@v = api-version=2026-11-01

### 1. Is it ready? Needs no api-version. Ready means Postgres answers; the provider is not part of readiness.
GET {{payments}}/health/ready

### 2. Sign in as a traveller. Later requests read the token from here.
# @name travellerLogin
POST {{keycloak}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=viatica-cli&username=traveller1&password=viatica

@travellerToken = {{travellerLogin.response.body.access_token}}

### 3. Tokenise a test card (PAY-1). The number goes to the simulator and nowhere else.
# The answer is a token, the brand and the last four digits. The token is what Booking carries in
# PaymentAuthorizationRequested. Spaces and hyphens in the number are fine. Change the expiry to a month ahead.
# @name tokenise
POST {{payments}}/payments/methods?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{travellerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "number": "4242 4242 4242 4242", "expMonth": 12, "expYear": 2030, "cvc": "123" }

@methodToken = {{tokenise.response.body.token}}

### 4. A card that will be declined later. It tokenises fine: the card decides the outcome when it is charged.
# 4000000000009995 is insufficient funds, 4000000000000002 a plain decline.
POST {{payments}}/payments/methods?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{travellerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "number": "4000000000009995", "expMonth": 12, "expYear": 2030, "cvc": "123" }

### 5. The two timeout cards. 4000009990000013 does the work and then loses the answer; 4000009990000021 hangs and does
# nothing. A payment on either one ends in the right single outcome, because Payments asks the simulator what it
# recorded under the operation's idempotency key before it decides (request 12 shows the key).
POST {{payments}}/payments/methods?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{travellerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "number": "4000009990000013", "expMonth": 12, "expYear": 2030, "cvc": "123" }

### 6. What a mistake looks like: a number that fails the Luhn check, a month of 13 and a short security code, all listed at once.
# The answer says which fields and why, and never repeats a value.
POST {{payments}}/payments/methods?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{travellerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "number": "4242424242424241", "expMonth": 13, "expYear": 2030, "cvc": "12" }

### 7. A number that is valid and is not a test card. The simulator refuses it, as Stripe's test mode refuses a real card.
# 400 PaymentMethodInvalid, with the simulator's code in innererror.
POST {{payments}}/payments/methods?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{travellerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "number": "4111111111111111", "expMonth": 12, "expYear": 2030, "cvc": "123" }

### 8. Sign in as an admin, to read a payment.
# @name adminLogin
POST {{keycloak}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=viatica-cli&username=admin1&password=viatica

@adminToken = {{adminLogin.response.body.access_token}}

### 9. Read a payment: its authorised, captured and refunded money, and every concluded operation with how it ended.
# Replace the id with a payment id from a PaymentAuthorized event. 404 PaymentNotFound otherwise. No card data.
GET {{payments}}/payments/0192f0c4-7a3b-7cde-8f00-4f7c2b1d9e01?{{v}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### The simulator ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 10. The test cards, and what each does.
GET {{psp}}/admin/cards

### 11. The live knobs and their defaults.
GET {{psp}}/admin/knobs

### 12. Ask the simulator what it recorded under an idempotency key (PAY-3). This is the call Payments makes after a timeout.
# The key of an operation is {tripId:N}:{kind}:{operationId:N}, for example
# 0192f0c47a3b7cde8f004f7c2b1d9e01:capture:0192f0c47a3b7cde8f004f7c2b1d9e02.
# 404 resource_missing means the call never reached the provider, or it hung without working.
GET {{psp}}/v1/operations?idempotency_key=0192f0c47a3b7cde8f004f7c2b1d9e01:capture:0192f0c47a3b7cde8f004f7c2b1d9e02

### 13. Every authorisation the simulator holds, with the trip it was made for in its metadata.
GET {{psp}}/v1/payment_intents

### 14. Make the provider slow for a while: every call takes about two seconds. Payments gives it five.
PATCH {{psp}}/admin/knobs
Content-Type: application/json

{ "LatencyMedianMs": 2000, "LatencySigma": 0.3 }

### 15. Make every call do its work and lose the answer, and take the lookup down too. Payments can then decide
# nothing, and keeps trying. Put the knobs back with request 17 and it concludes by itself, once.
PATCH {{psp}}/admin/knobs
Content-Type: application/json

{ "TimeoutRate": 1.0, "PerformedRate": 1.0, "LookupOutage": true }

### 16. A provider outage: every call answers 503 at once.
PATCH {{psp}}/admin/knobs
Content-Type: application/json

{ "Outage": true }

### 17. Put every knob back.
POST {{psp}}/admin/knobs/reset
